content.text:
`createParents` is now an optional field on `MakeDirectory`. Leave it unset and each provider keeps its own default.

| Value | Daytona | Modal |
|---|---|---|
| Omitted | Creates missing parents. An existing directory returns the provider error. | Creates missing parents. An existing directory succeeds. |
| `true` | Same as Daytona’s default. | Same as Modal’s default. |
| `false` | Rejected before Daytona is called. The error field is `create_parents`. | The parent must already exist. An existing directory returns an error. |

```ts
await sandbox.filesystem.makeDirectory("/tmp/kit-files");
await sandbox.filesystem.makeDirectory("/tmp/kit-files/nested", {
  createParents: false,
});
```

```go
err := instance.Filesystem().MakeDirectory(ctx, "/tmp/kit-files", nil)
err = instance.Filesystem().MakeDirectory(ctx, "/tmp/kit-files/nested", &sandbox.MakeDirectoryOptions{
	CreateParents: sandbox.Value(false),
})
```

The second call is the Modal setting. On Daytona it fails in Kit with `create_parents` before the provider is contacted.

`createParents` reads like a second verb next to `makeDirectory`. The other flags on this handle are single words: `recursive`, `force`, `wait`.

I would name the field `parents`.

```ts
await sandbox.filesystem.makeDirectory("/tmp/kit-files/nested", {
  parents: false,
});
```

```go
err := instance.Filesystem().MakeDirectory(ctx, "/tmp/kit-files/nested", &sandbox.MakeDirectoryOptions{
	Parents: sandbox.Value(false),
})
```

An omitted `parents` still means the provider default. `false` still means the parent directory must already exist. Modal’s own SDK calls this `createParents`; that name can stay inside the Modal adapter.

