content.text:
A file timeout and a bad local path now fail in different ways. These are the calls a user actually writes.

## Timeout

TypeScript read, write, and copy send the timeout to Daytona as whole seconds. `2_000` becomes `2`. `1_500` is rejected before Daytona is called. The kind is invalid argument, the field is `timeout`, and the operation is `read_file`, `write_file`, `copy_from_local`, or `copy_to_local`.

```ts
await sandbox.filesystem.readFile("/tmp/note.txt", { timeout: 2_000 });
// Daytona downloadFile("/tmp/note.txt", 2)

await sandbox.filesystem.readFile("/tmp/note.txt", { timeout: 1_500 });
// SandboxError { kind: "invalid_argument", field: "timeout", operation: "read_file" }
```

The same `1_500` is a real deadline on every other file call. Listing a directory with that timeout aborts at 1.5 seconds. Daytona receives no seconds argument for that call.

```ts
await sandbox.filesystem.listDirectory("/tmp", { timeout: 1_500 });
// Kit aborts the call at 1.5s. Daytona listFiles("/tmp") gets no timeout argument.
```

Go Daytona has no seconds argument on any file method. `1500 * time.Millisecond` and `2 * time.Second` are both context deadlines. The call is canceled when the context ends.

```go
data, err := instance.Filesystem().ReadFile(ctx, "/tmp/note.txt", &sandbox.FileReadOptions{
    Timeout: sandbox.Duration(1500 * time.Millisecond),
})
// The context ends at 1.5s. DownloadFile is called with that context and no seconds value.

entries, err := instance.Filesystem().ListDirectory(ctx, "/tmp", &sandbox.ListDirectoryOptions{
    Timeout: sandbox.Duration(2 * time.Second),
})
// Same shape: the context ends at 2s.
```

Modal is the same shape in both languages. The context or the abort signal is the deadline, including 1.5 seconds.

`timeout: 0` adds no deadline of its own. An omitted timeout uses the client deadline.

## Local path

`copyFromLocal` checks the file on your machine before Daytona is called.

```ts
await sandbox.filesystem.copyFromLocal("./missing.txt", "/tmp/note.txt");
// SandboxError { kind: "not_found", field: "local_path", operation: "copy_from_local" }

await sandbox.filesystem.copyFromLocal("./notes", "/tmp/note.txt");
// ./notes is a directory
// SandboxError { kind: "invalid_argument", field: "local_path", operation: "copy_from_local" }
```

```go
err := instance.Filesystem().CopyFromLocal(ctx, "missing.txt", "/tmp/note.txt", nil)
// kind not_found, field local_path, operation copy_from_local

err = instance.Filesystem().CopyFromLocal(ctx, "notes", "/tmp/note.txt", nil)
// notes is a directory
// kind invalid_argument, field local_path, operation copy_from_local
```

`copyToLocal` allows a destination that does not exist yet, because the write creates that file. A destination that is already a directory is rejected first.

```ts
await sandbox.filesystem.copyToLocal("/tmp/note.txt", "./notes");
// ./notes is a directory
// SandboxError { kind: "invalid_argument", field: "local_path", operation: "copy_to_local" }
```

If the download reaches the local write and that write fails, the field stays `local_path`. A missing parent is not found. A permission failure is an invalid argument.

```go
err := instance.Filesystem().CopyToLocal(ctx, "/tmp/note.txt", "missing-dir/note.txt", nil)
// kind not_found, field local_path, operation copy_to_local

err = instance.Filesystem().CopyToLocal(ctx, "/tmp/note.txt", "/root/note.txt", nil)
// kind invalid_argument, field local_path, operation copy_to_local
```

`1_500` is 1.5 seconds. On a TypeScript Daytona read, write, or copy, Kit has to turn that into the seconds argument those methods take, and it only forwards a value that divides evenly.

```ts
await sandbox.filesystem.readFile("/tmp/note.txt", { timeout: 2_000 });
// 2000 / 1000 = 2
// sandbox.fs.downloadFile("/tmp/note.txt", 2)

await sandbox.filesystem.readFile("/tmp/note.txt", { timeout: 1_500 });
// 1500 / 1000 = 1.5
// rejected: kind invalid_argument, field timeout, operation read_file
```

Daytona documents that argument as seconds. `2` is two seconds. There is no `1.5` that Kit can pass while keeping the same length you wrote. Sending `1` would stop the call at one second. Sending `2` would allow two seconds. Kit rejects `1_500` so the number Daytona receives stays the deadline from the call.

The same `1_500` is accepted on a list, a directory create, a metadata read, or a delete, and on every Go file call. Those methods have no seconds argument. Kit cancels the context or the abort signal at 1.5 seconds.

```ts
await sandbox.filesystem.listDirectory("/tmp", { timeout: 1_500 });
// Daytona listFiles("/tmp") is called with no seconds argument.
// Kit aborts the call at 1.5 seconds.
```

```go
data, err := instance.Filesystem().ReadFile(ctx, "/tmp/note.txt", &sandbox.FileReadOptions{
    Timeout: sandbox.Duration(1500 * time.Millisecond),
})
// DownloadFile(ctx, "/tmp/note.txt", nil)
// ctx ends at 1.5 seconds.
```

Keep the `timeout` field on the file option messages. It is Kit's deadline for that call, the same role it has on start, stop, and create.

```525:538:proto/kit/sandbox/v1/sandbox.proto
// FileReadOptions configures reading one file. Nil timeout inherits Config.Timeout.
message FileReadOptions {
  google.protobuf.Duration timeout = 1;
}
// ...
message FileCopyOptions {
  google.protobuf.Duration timeout = 1;
}
```

The public layer turns that duration into a context deadline or an abort signal before the provider runs. An omitted value uses the client deadline. Explicit zero adds no deadline of its own.

```go
data, err := instance.Filesystem().ReadFile(ctx, "/tmp/note.txt", &sandbox.FileReadOptions{
    Timeout: sandbox.Duration(1500 * time.Millisecond),
})
// The context ends at 1.5 seconds.
```

```ts
await sandbox.filesystem.listDirectory("/tmp", { timeout: 1_500 });
// The abort signal fires at 1.5 seconds.
```

Daytona's seconds argument is a separate step, and only TypeScript read, write, and copy have one. `2_000` is forwarded as `2`. `1_500` is rejected because 1.5 seconds cannot be passed as a whole number of seconds. The proto field stays, because the caller still asked for a 1.5 second deadline on every other file call.

Removing it would leave file calls with only the client deadline. A two-second read and a thirty-second list on the same client would share one limit.

Two filesystem bugs are still in the code. Both show up as a result that does not match the deadline or the error the call is supposed to return.

**TypeScript Modal leaves a local copy error unclassified.** Go Modal turns a missing local file into not found with the field `local_path`. The TypeScript Modal adapter does not. `copyFromLocal` throws Node's own `ENOENT` or `EISDIR`, and the mapper only recognizes Modal error types, so the caller gets kind `unknown` and an empty field.

```ts
await sandbox.filesystem.copyFromLocal("./missing.txt", "/tmp/note.txt");
// SandboxError { kind: "unknown", field: "", operation: "copy_from_local" }
```

```go
err := instance.Filesystem().CopyFromLocal(ctx, "missing.txt", "/tmp/note.txt", nil)
// kind not_found, field local_path, operation copy_from_local
```

The same gap applies to `copyToLocal` when the local write fails. The fix is the same check Daytona already does: stat the local path before the Modal call, and map a Node `ENOENT` to not found and a directory or permission error to invalid argument, with the field `local_path`.

**An omitted TypeScript Daytona read, write, or copy timeout becomes 30 minutes.** Those Daytona methods default the seconds argument to `30 * 60` when it is left out. Kit only forwards a number when the caller set one. With no per-call timeout, Daytona stops the transfer at 30 minutes even when the client deadline is longer, or when the caller set no Kit deadline at all.

```ts
await sandbox.filesystem.readFile("/tmp/note.txt");
// Kit adds no extra deadline.
// Daytona still calls downloadFile(path) and applies its 30 minute default.
```

```ts
await sandbox.filesystem.readFile("/tmp/note.txt", { timeout: 0 });
// This one is forwarded as 0, which means no Daytona limit.
```

Passing `0` when the Kit timeout is omitted makes Daytona add no limit of its own. The abort signal remains the client deadline.

One smaller Go Daytona issue: `copyToLocal` accepts a destination whose parent directory does not exist, downloads the remote file, and only then fails the local write. The error is not found with the field `local_path`, but the bytes have already been transferred. Checking that the parent directory exists before `DownloadFile` avoids that.

